# Every text file is LF in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# **`Q-73`: a fresh clone of this repository failed its own suite, and no existing working
# tree could see it.** There was no `.gitattributes`, `core.autocrlf` is true on Windows, and
# every generated report is committed with LF - so a checkout rewrote them CRLF, and
# `crates/game-console/tests/dump.rs` asked whether `turns.md` contains `"\n# Turn 1\n"`.
# It does not: it contains `"\r\n# Turn 1\r\n"`. The blob is identical either way, both trees
# are status-clean, and the difference exists only after checkout.
#
# **Nobody had hit it because no existing tree's copy was checked out** - every one was
# written by the generator, which writes LF. And CI could not: the gate runs on Linux where
# `autocrlf` is off, and the only Windows job builds without testing. The first push from a
# fresh Windows clone would have failed the gate for a reason unrelated to the change.
#
# **Settling the endings rather than making the parse tolerant**, which was the choice. Every
# generator here writes LF, so LF is the canonical form and the rest is checkout doing
# something to it. Tolerance would have to be added to every parser that reads a generated
# file, including the next one somebody writes, and this is one line that cannot be forgotten.
#
# `text=auto` leaves binaries alone: git detects them and this does not apply. The only
# extensions here that sometimes want CRLF are PowerShell scripts, which read LF fine, and
# there are no `.bat` or `.cmd` files.
#
# Found by the quality lens, which built a fresh clone to establish a baseline and got a
# failure this tree cannot produce.
* text=auto eol=lf
